I recently asked a question <http://stackoverflow.com/questions/42704919/what-is-the-tidyverse-method-for-splitting-a-df-by-multiple-columns/42705616#42705616> on SO about the tidyverse method for splitting a df by multiple columns.

In the example I provided, I wanted to split a df by two cols and obtain a summary() output for each subset of the df.

My initial instinct was to use purrr::by_slice(), but that has been deprecated <https://github.com/hadley/purrr/issues/270>. The suggested solution uses group_by followed by do(), resulting in a list-col with the summaries:
library(tidyverse)

library(magrittr)

mtcars_summary <-

    mtcars %>%
    select(1:3) %>%
       mutate(GRP_A = sample(LETTERS[1:2],  n(), replace = TRUE),
           GRP_B = sample(c(1:2), n(), replace = TRUE)) %>%
    group_by(GRP_A,  GRP_B) %>%
    do(SUMMARY = summary(.))

